      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of different orders in which Jordan's relay team can run, we need to consider the constraints and the permutations among the team members. Jordan will always run the fourth lap, so we only need to arrange the other three team members—let's call them Alex, Ben, and Carol—in the first three positions.\n\nThe number of ways to arrange three people in three positions is given by the factorial of 3, denoted as \\(3!\\). The factorial of a number \\(n\\) is the product of all positive integers up to \\(n\\). Therefore, we calculate \\(3!\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 6 different ways to arrange Alex, Ben, and Carol in the first three positions. Since Jordan's position is fixed as the fourth lap, the total number of different orders in which the team members can run the race is simply the number of ways to arrange the three other runners, which is 6.\n\nThus, the number of different orders in which the four team members can run, assuming that Jordan runs the fourth lap, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
